{
  "requirement": "The harness represents a domain `D::NodeId` to the agent as an opaque JSON string scoped to the conversation. It retains the IDs of the current node, supplied input nodes, and nodes introduced by tool results. Tools that return nodes give the agent references to those IDs. Tools that accept a node resolve its reference to the same ID. An unknown reference produces a tool error.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover each obligation in `node_reference`.\n\n**Opaque JSON string, scoped to the conversation**\n- `agent/project_sources.rs#mod[tests]/fn[node_references_resolve_only_within_their_conversation]` uses two separate `Pages` instances. The same reference string, `n0`, resolves to 20 in one and 10 in the other. A reference registered only in the first (`peer`) is an error in the second.\n- `agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agent_uses_domain_source_tools_in_independent_executions]` asserts that every file reference in the `list_sources` output is a string starting with `n`. Its `read_node` call on a reference returned by `list_sources` resolves to the right snapshot.\n- `agent/tests.rs#fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` runs two concurrent conversations. Each reads its own node by reference, and neither conversation's history contains the other's contents.\n\n**Retained IDs: current node, supplied input nodes, nodes introduced by tool results**\n- In the concurrent test, the alpha conversation reads the current node as `n0` and gets its contents. The beta conversation, built with `contents_of_successors`, reads the supplied successor as `n1`. The dependencies include both Node(1) and Node(2).\n- `agent/tests.rs#fn[graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery]` calls `outgoing_edges` from `n0`. It then calls `read_node` on the returned `n1` in a later turn, which resolves to Node(2). The test asserts the resulting dependency.\n- `agent/tests.rs#fn[agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets]` covers a second label's result. A node introduced by a tool result, `n1` (node 3), is then passed back to `outgoing_edges`. It resolves to the same ID, because the test asserts `OutgoingEdges(3, 7, …)`.\n- The configured source test reads nodes introduced by `list_sources` and by `search_text` results (`hit[\"node\"]`).\n\n**Tools that return nodes give references to those IDs**\n- The edge-tool test asserts the result `{\"targets\":[\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n1\"]}` for edges `[3,2,3]`. This shows that a repeated ID gets the same reference. In the second scenario `[3]` yields `[\"n1\"]`.\n- `agent/pages.rs#impl[Pages<N, L>]/fn[register]` is the implementation for this, and the tests exercise it through these results.\n\n**Tools that accept a node resolve its reference to the same ID**\n- The tests assert the resolved IDs through the recorded dependencies, as listed above.\n- `Pages::node` is also checked directly in the project_sources test.\n\n**An unknown reference produces a tool error**\n- The edge-tool test sends `outgoing_edges` with `node: \"unknown\"`. It asserts that `is_error` is true with the content \"unknown node reference\". It also asserts that no graph lookup happened and no dependency was recorded.\n- The configured source test sends `read_node` with `unknown` and asserts that the output contains \"unknown node\".\n- The project_sources test asserts that a reference from another conversation is an error.\n\nThese tests together exercise every clause with real assertions. I found no gap.",
  "references": "770f92be066c77c650c0566bf962a5dd3f3dd85f4e43dac6c643e6a5d03acd32",
